<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Paskevich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Fyodorovich Paskevich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Paskevich was a Russian field marshal who won the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 1826–1828 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1828–1829. He captured Erivan in 1827, for which he was named Count of Erivan, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> gave the khanate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1831 he crushed the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, then governed Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as viceroy until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich.jpg" alt="Ivan Paskevich" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87_%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Paskevich was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> on 19 May 1782, the son of a Zaporozhian Cossack gentry family and a Belarusian noblewoman. He was educated at the Page Corps and in 1800 received a commission in the Guards and the post of aide-de-camp to the tsar. His first active service was at Austerlitz in 1805. From 1807 to 1812 he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and became a general officer in his thirtieth year. In 1812–1814 he commanded the 26th infantry division and fought at Borodino, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1826–1828 Paskevich commanded the 1st Guard Infantry, in which the future Nicholas I served, and Nicholas came to call him father-commander. In the spring after the war began he replaced Aleksey Petrovich Yermolov as chief commander. Under him the Erivan and Nakhichevan Khanates were conquered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and the tsar gave him the title Count of Erivan, a million rubles and a diamond-mounted sword. He then led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> front in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> and was made field marshal at forty-seven.</p><p>In June 1831, after Field Marshal von Diebitsch died, Paskevich took command of the Russian troops crushing the Polish November Uprising. After a slow advance he redeemed his reputation at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, which ended Polish hopes of independence. He was created Prince of Warsaw and namestnik of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. In 1848 he commanded the Russian troops sent to aid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848">Hungarian Revolution</a> and forced the Hungarians to surrender at Világos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the period under Paskevich is known as the Paskevich Night and remembered for political repressions, economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>, though he also promoted industry, highways, railways and fortresses. A decree prepared with his participation in 1846 barred landlords from removing peasants holding more than three morgen from the land. In 1854 he took command of the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the opening phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, laid siege to Silistria and advised abandoning the campaign because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> threatened to intervene. Wounded on 9 June, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 1 February 1856, and his titles ended with his only son in 1903.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Paskevich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The governor Field Marshal <b>Ivan Paskevich</b> directly controlled the Administrative Council.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Russo-Turkish War Russian forces under <b>Paskevich</b> stormed Akhaltsikhe in 1828, and the Treaty of Adrianople in 1829 gave the northern part to the Russian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/childir-eyalet">Childir Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian troops under <b>Ivan Paskevich</b> captured Erivan in 1827, and the Treaty of Turkmenchay in 1828 ceded the khanate to the Russian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/erivan-khanate">Erivan Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Poltava, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.59,34.55&amp;year=1830" aria-label="Poltava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5344 436 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ivan Paskevich"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5794 706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5794 706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.59,34.55&amp;year=1830">What was here in 1830? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
